  "content": {
    "id": "p16-q8-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "8",
    "prompt": "If $p$ and $q$ are positive integers such that\n$$\\frac{7}{10}<\\frac{p}{q}<\\frac{11}{15},$$\nthen find the smallest possible value of $q$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$q=7$.\n\nThe fraction $\\frac{5}{7}$ lies between the bounds; no smaller positive denominator works.",
      "worked": "Since $q>0$, the numerator must satisfy $\\frac{7q}{10}<p<\\frac{11q}{15}$.\n\nFor $q=1$: $\\frac{7}{10}<p<\\frac{11}{15}$.\n\nFor $q=2$: $\\frac{7}{5}<p<\\frac{22}{15}$.\n\nFor $q=3$: $\\frac{21}{10}<p<\\frac{11}{5}$.\n\nFor $q=4$: $\\frac{14}{5}<p<\\frac{44}{15}$.\n\nFor $q=5$: $\\frac{7}{2}<p<\\frac{11}{3}$.\n\nFor $q=6$: $\\frac{21}{5}<p<\\frac{22}{5}$.\n\nNone of these intervals contains an integer. For $q=7$, choose $p=5$. Using a common denominator,\n$$\\frac{7}{10}=\\frac{147}{210}<\\frac{150}{210}=\\frac{5}{7}<\\frac{154}{210}=\\frac{11}{15}.$$\nTherefore, the smallest possible denominator is $q=7$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 100
  },
